# gcr3 -- the previous gcr generation, stripped to the two libraries
# gnome-keyring cannot start without.
#
# THIS PACKAGE EXISTS FOR TWO .so FILES AND NOTHING ELSE. gnome-keyring 48.0's
# meson.build (lines 37-39) asks unconditionally for
#
#     gck-1        >= 3.3.4
#     gcr-base-3   >= 3.27.90
#     libgcrypt    >= 1.2.2
#
# and gcr-4 STRUCTURALLY CANNOT satisfy the first two: it installs gck-2 and
# gcr-base-4, which are different names, not newer versions of the same ones.
# So the pair is not a transition that someone forgot to finish -- it is two
# APIs with two sets of consumers. pkgs/gcr is the gtk4 generation the live
# session links; this is the compatibility half.
#
# Not an LFS package, so it is pinned here rather than in versions.env.
# BLFS 12.4 pins 3.41.2 on its own page, separate from the gcr-4 one.
SRC_URL=https://download.gnome.org/sources/gcr/3.41/gcr-3.41.2.tar.xz
SRC_SHA256=bad10f3c553a0e1854649ab59c5b2434da22ca1a54ae6138f1f53961567e1ab7
SRC_DIR=gcr-3.41.2

# ==============================================================================
# -Dssh_agent=false: THE COLLISION, RESOLVED ON THIS SIDE
# ==============================================================================
# gcr-3 and gcr-4 coexist because upstream versioned every installed name --
# libgck-1 against libgck-2, libgcr-base-3 against libgcr-4, headers in gck-1
# and gcr-3 against gck-2 and gcr-4, gcr-viewer against gcr-viewer-gtk4,
# gcr-ssh-askpass against gcr4-ssh-askpass. EXACTLY ONE NAME WAS MISSED:
#
#   gcr-3.41.2/gcr/meson.build:251   executable('gcr-ssh-agent', install_dir: libexecdir)
#   gcr-4.4.0.1/gcr/meson.build:260  executable('gcr-ssh-agent', install_dir: libexecdir)
#
# Both land on /usr/libexec/gcr-ssh-agent, and two packages owning one path is
# a hard install error with no override. Upstream's claim that the two are
# coinstallable is true only with a flag its own build instructions pass
# without comment.
#
# GCR-4 KEEPS THE AGENT, THIS ONE GIVES IT UP -- THE OPPOSITE OF BLFS, WHICH
# PASSES -D ssh_agent=false ON GCR-4. That is correct for BLFS and wrong here,
# because a book resolves a collision in favour of ITS incumbent and the
# incumbency is inverted in this tree: gcr-4 is what gnome-shell and
# gnome-control-center link, while this package is wanted for two libraries.
# The agent belongs with the generation that runs the session.
#
# ==============================================================================
# -Dgtk=false: REMOVING THE COLLISION'S NEIGHBOURHOOD ENTIRELY
# ==============================================================================
# The option defaults TRUE and builds the GTK-3 UI library, gcr-viewer and
# gcr-prompter. None of it is wanted: gnome-keyring needs gck-1 and gcr-base-3,
# both of which are non-UI, and the certificate dialogs the session actually
# shows come from gcr-4.
#
# CONFIRMED BY UPSTREAM'S OWN RELEASE NOTE RATHER THAN BY GREPPING FOR ABSENCE.
# gnome-keyring 48.0's NEWS says "meson: Remove dependency on gcr-ui-3 [!91]" --
# they deliberately dropped it, which is positive evidence rather than a failed
# search. Its meson.build asks for no UI package and its sources include
# gcr-base.h and gcr-unlock-options.h, both from gcr-base-3.
#
# The payoff is larger than the flag: with the UI gone this package has NO GTK3
# EDGE AT ALL, which keeps gtk3 at exactly one consumer in the whole
# distribution. It also removes the collision by construction rather than by
# switch -- with no UI and no agent, what remains cannot collide with gcr-4
# because every surviving name is version-suffixed.
#
# ==============================================================================
# THE REST
# ==============================================================================
# -Dsystemd=disabled. 'auto', and it would resolve off anyway through the same
# libsystemd-AND-systemd conjunction gcr-4 has; named rather than left to a
# compatibility symlink. With ssh_agent off there are no units to install
# regardless, so this is belt and braces.
#
# -Dgpg_path=/usr/bin/gpg. Unset, meson runs find_program('gpg2', 'gpg') with
# no required: false and bakes the resolved path in. gnupg installs gpg and not
# gpg2, so the probe succeeds on its second candidate; naming it removes the
# dependence on probe order.
#
# -Dgtk_doc=false. Defaults TRUE and wants gtk-doc, which is not packaged.
#
# -Dintrospection=false, AND AN EARLIER DRAFT OF THIS FILE HAD IT true ON THE
# GROUNDS THAT "THE TYPELIB IS CHEAP". It is not cheap here, and the build said
# so. gcr-3 has NO vapi option -- gcr-4 added one -- and
# gck/meson.build:144 calls gnome.generate_vapi() INSIDE the
# `if get_option('introspection')` block, unconditionally within it. So
# introspection drags in vapigen, vapigen comes from vala, and vala is not
# packaged:
#
#     ERROR: Program 'vapigen' not found or not executable
#
# There is no flag that separates the two in this version. The choice is
# therefore introspection-plus-vala or neither, and neither is right: this
# package exists for two .so files that gnome-keyring LINKS, and nothing
# reaches gck-1 or gcr-base-3 through a typelib. gcr-4 carries Gcr-4 and Gck-2
# for the JavaScript consumers.
#
# This is the second thing gcr-4 fixed that gcr-3 still has (the other being
# the unguarded ssh-add lookup this recipe patches), and both were found by
# building rather than reading.
#
# NOTE: this recipe has no -Dcrypto. That option exists only in gcr-4; gcr-3
# takes libgcrypt unconditionally, which is the same backend the rest of the
# keyring stack uses.
MESON_ARGS="-Dssh_agent=false -Dgtk=false -Dsystemd=disabled \
-Dgpg_path=/usr/bin/gpg -Dgtk_doc=false -Dintrospection=false"
